<InsightArticle title={article.title} description={article.description} date={article.date} type={article.type} readTime={article.readTime} snapshot="2026-11-14" standfirst="Article 50 of the EU AI Act has applied since 2 August 2026, and an amendment in force since 27 July 2026 gives providers of generative AI systems already on the market until 2 December 2026 to meet its marking duty.">
  <p>Article 50 of the EU AI Act requires providers of AI systems that generate synthetic audio, images, video or text to make sure the output is "marked in a machine-readable format and detectable as artificially generated or manipulated". The Act's general date of application, 2 August 2026, covers that article, and an amendment that entered into force on 27 July 2026 gives providers whose systems were already on the market before 2 August 2026 until 2 December 2026 to comply with that marking duty: 18 days after we read the texts on 14 November 2026.</p>
  <p>We read the Official Journal text of <a href={links.act} rel="noopener">Regulation (EU) 2024/1689</a>, the AI Act, and of the <a href={links.omnibus} rel="noopener">amending Regulation (EU) 2026/1744</a>, which the Commission calls the AI Omnibus, in full on 14 November 2026. EUR-Lex did not return a page to a plain download, so we read the same Official Journal files from the EU Publications Office (<a href={links.actCopy} rel="noopener">AI Act copy</a>, <a href={links.omnibusCopy} rel="noopener">amendment copy</a>). This piece reports what those texts and the European Commission's own pages say. It is not legal advice, and it does not say whether any particular tool, service or post is covered.</p>

  <h2>Who counts as a provider and who counts as a deployer</h2>
  <p>Article 50 splits its duties between two roles defined in Article 3. A provider (Article 3(3)) is a person, company or public body that develops an AI system or general-purpose AI model, or has one developed, and places it on the market or puts it into service under its own name or trademark, "whether for payment or free of charge". A deployer (Article 3(4)) is anyone using an AI system under its authority, except where the system is used in the course of a personal non-professional activity. Article 2(10) repeats that point from the other side: the Regulation does not apply to obligations of deployers who are natural persons using AI systems for a purely personal non-professional activity.</p>
  <p>In plain terms, the company that builds and offers an image generator is its provider, and a newsroom, agency or business that uses it to make published material is a deployer. Article 2(12) also matters for open models: the general exclusion for AI systems released under free and open-source licences does not apply to a system that falls under Article 50.</p>

  <h2>The duties side by side</h2>
  <div class="table-wrap"><table>
    <caption>Source: Regulation (EU) 2024/1689, Articles 3, 50 and 111, as amended by Regulation (EU) 2026/1744. Paraphrases are ours; read 14 November 2026. Not legal advice.</caption>
    <thead><tr><th>Duty</th><th>Who</th><th>Content or system</th><th>Exception stated in the text</th><th>Where</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Make sure outputs are marked in a machine-readable format and detectable as artificially generated or manipulated</td><td>Provider</td><td>Synthetic audio, image, video or text, including from general-purpose AI systems</td><td>To the extent the system performs an assistive function for standard editing, or does not substantially alter the deployer's input or its meaning; or where authorised by law for criminal offences</td><td>Art. 50(2)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Disclose that the content has been artificially generated or manipulated</td><td>Deployer</td><td>Image, audio or video content constituting a deep fake</td><td>Use authorised by law for criminal offences. For an evidently artistic, creative, satirical, fictional or analogous work, the duty is limited (not removed) to disclosing the content exists, in a way that does not hamper the work</td><td>Art. 50(4), first subparagraph</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Disclose that the text has been artificially generated or manipulated</td><td>Deployer</td><td>Text published with the purpose of informing the public on matters of public interest</td><td>Use authorised by law for criminal offences; or the content has had human review or editorial control and a person holds editorial responsibility</td><td>Art. 50(4), second subparagraph</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Tell people they are interacting with an AI system</td><td>Provider</td><td>Systems intended to interact directly with people</td><td>Where obvious to a reasonably well-informed, observant and circumspect person; certain law enforcement systems</td><td>Art. 50(1)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Inform people exposed to the system</td><td>Deployer</td><td>Emotion recognition or biometric categorisation systems</td><td>Uses permitted by law to detect, prevent or investigate criminal offences</td><td>Art. 50(3)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Give the information clearly and distinguishably, at the latest at first interaction or exposure, meeting accessibility rules</td><td>Both</td><td>Everything in paragraphs 1 to 4</td><td>None stated</td><td>Art. 50(5)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Comply with Article 50(2) by 2 December 2026</td><td>Provider</td><td>Generative systems placed on the market before 2 August 2026</td><td>Applies only to the marking duty in 50(2)</td><td>Art. 111(4), added by Reg. 2026/1744</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
  <p>Paragraphs 1 and 3 are about chatbots and emotion or biometric systems rather than generated content, so they get one row each here and no more.</p>

  <h2>What "machine-readable" and "detectable" mean in the text</h2>
  <p>Article 50(2) does not list a technology. It sets a standard: providers must ensure their technical solutions are {qualityTest}, taking into account the specificities and limitations of different types of content, the costs of implementation and "the generally acknowledged state of the art, as may be reflected in relevant technical standards". The phrase "as far as this is technically feasible" is part of the duty, and the reference to different types of content means the text allows for differences between, say, a photo and a paragraph of text.</p>
  <p>The examples sit in recital 133, one of the numbered explanatory paragraphs printed before the articles. It says the techniques should take into account available techniques or a combination of them, "such as watermarks, metadata identifications, cryptographic methods for proving provenance and authenticity of content, logging methods, fingerprints or other techniques, as may be appropriate". The same recital says these methods can be built in at the level of the AI system or at the level of the model, including general-purpose AI models, which can help the downstream provider of a system meet the duty. Recitals explain the articles; the duty itself is in Article 50(2).</p>
  <p>The standard-editing exception in 50(2) applies "to the extent" a system performs an assistive function for standard editing or does not substantially alter the input data provided by the deployer "or the semantics thereof". The Act does not define standard editing in Article 50. The Commission says its guidelines give examples of exceptions such as standard editing; we have not summarised those examples because we read only the Commission's page about the guidelines, not the guidelines document.</p>

  <h2>Deep fakes, satire and public-interest text</h2>
  <p>Article 3(60) defines a deep fake as AI-generated or manipulated image, audio or video content that resembles existing persons, objects, places, entities or events "and would falsely appear to a person to be authentic or truthful". Text is not in that definition, which is why the second subparagraph of Article 50(4) deals with text separately.</p>
  <p>The artistic exception does not remove the duty. Where a deep fake forms part of an evidently artistic, creative, satirical, fictional or analogous work or programme, the text limits the duty "to disclosure of the existence of such generated or manipulated content in an appropriate manner that does not hamper the display or enjoyment of the work". A disclosure is still required; the text changes how it is made. Recital 134 links this to the Charter rights to freedom of expression and freedom of the arts and sciences, "subject to appropriate safeguards for the rights and freedoms of third parties".</p>
  <p>The text duty has two conditions for its exception, joined by "and": the AI-generated content has undergone human review or editorial control, and a natural or legal person holds editorial responsibility for the publication. The duty covers text published with the purpose of informing the public on matters of public interest. The Act does not define "matters of public interest" in Article 50; the Commission says its guidelines give a definition of AI-generated text on matters of public interest.</p>

  <h2>Two dates apply, and the amendment set the second</h2>
  <div class="table-wrap"><table>
    <caption>Sources: Regulation (EU) 2024/1689 Article 113; Regulation (EU) 2026/1744 Article 1 points (20), (39) and (40) and Article 4; proposal COM(2025) 836; European Commission AI Act page. Read 14 November 2026.</caption>
    <thead><tr><th>Date</th><th>What happened or applies</th><th>Status on 14 November 2026</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>1 August 2024</td><td>AI Act entered into force (Commission page)</td><td>In force</td></tr>
      <tr><td>19 November 2025</td><td>Commission proposal COM(2025) 836 proposed a transitional period to 2 February 2027 for Article 50(2)</td><td>Superseded by the adopted text</td></tr>
      <tr><td>7 May 2026</td><td>Political agreement on the AI Omnibus (Commission page)</td><td>Done</td></tr>
      <tr><td>8 July 2026</td><td>Regulation (EU) 2026/1744 signed; published in the Official Journal on 24 July 2026</td><td>Adopted</td></tr>
      <tr><td>27 July 2026</td><td>Regulation (EU) 2026/1744 entered into force (Commission page; the text sets the third day after publication)</td><td>In force</td></tr>
      <tr><td>2 August 2026</td><td>General date of application of the AI Act, which covers Article 50</td><td>Applies</td></tr>
      <tr><td>2 December 2026</td><td>Deadline in Article 111(4) for providers of generative systems placed on the market before 2 August 2026 to comply with Article 50(2)</td><td>Upcoming</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
  <p>Article 113 of the AI Act says it "shall apply from 2 August 2026" and then lists exceptions with other dates. Article 50 sits in Chapter IV, and none of the exceptions names Chapter IV, either in the original text or after the 2026 amendment, which changed points (a) and (c) of that list and added a point (d) for other articles. The Commission's <a href={links.quickFacts} rel="noopener">quick facts page</a> and its <a href={links.guidelines} rel="noopener">guidelines page</a> both say Article 50 applies from 2 August 2026.</p>
  <p>The grace period covers one duty. Article 111(4), added by Article 1 point (39) of the amendment, says providers of systems generating synthetic audio, image, video or text "that have been placed on the market before 2 August 2026 shall take the necessary steps in order to comply with Article 50(2) by 2 December 2026". It mentions only paragraph 2, the marking duty, and only systems already on the market before that date. Recital 38 of the amendment calls it "a transitional period of four months". The <a href={links.proposal} rel="noopener">Commission's proposal</a> had asked for six months, to 2 February 2027; the adopted text shortened it.</p>
  <p>On deep fakes made before the rules applied, the Commission's quick facts page says "no mandatory retroactive labelling but encouraged". That line comes from the Commission's summary page, not from the text of the Act.</p>

  <h2>The amendment rewrote paragraph 7, not the duties</h2>
  <p>We found one change to Article 50 itself in Regulation (EU) 2026/1744: Article 1 point (20) replaces paragraph 7. Paragraphs 1 to 6, which hold the duties above, are not amended by it. The original paragraph 7 had the AI Office encourage codes of practice on "detection and labelling" and let the Commission approve such codes by implementing act. The new wording gives the encouraging role to the Commission, adds marking to the list ("detection, marking and labelling"), and says the Commission, taking utmost account of the AI Board's opinion, shall assess whether adherence to those codes is adequate to ensure compliance with paragraphs 2 and 4. If it deems a code inadequate, the Commission may adopt an implementing act setting common rules.</p>
  <p>Two documents now sit under that paragraph, according to the Commission's pages. The <a href={links.code} rel="noopener">Code of Practice on Transparency of AI-generated Content</a>, which the Commission's quick facts page says was published in June 2026, has one section for providers on marking and detection and one for deployers on labelling deep fakes and text. The Commission's page calls adherence voluntary and says "the transparency requirements under article 50 of the AI Act are legal obligations". In its <a href={links.opinion} rel="noopener">opinion</a>, the Commission concluded on 8 July that the code adequately covers Articles 50(2), (4) and (5), while stating that "adherence to the code does not constitute conclusive evidence of compliance". The code page reports about 190 signatories by the end of July 2026, and the Commission says it has made a set of three optional icons that deployers may use as labels. The second document is the Commission's guidelines on Article 50, which its <a href={links.guidelines} rel="noopener">guidelines page</a> says cover scope, definitions and exceptions; providers and deployers that do not sign the code "will have to demonstrate compliance" for marking and labelling "through alternative equivalently adequate means", the page says.</p>

  <h2>Fines and the limits of this reading</h2>
  <p>Article 99(4) lists non-compliance with Article 50 among the breaches subject to administrative fines of up to EUR 15,000,000 or, for an undertaking, up to 3% of its total worldwide annual turnover for the preceding financial year, whichever is higher. Article 99(6) caps fines for SMEs, including start-ups, at whichever of those figures is lower, and the 2026 amendment adds paragraph 6a applying the lower figure to small mid-caps. Member States lay down the rules on penalties under Article 99(1). These are maximums written in the text; the Act does not say what any fine would be in a given case.</p>
  <p>Article 50(6) says paragraphs 1 to 4 do not affect the high-risk rules in Chapter III and are without prejudice to other transparency obligations in EU or national law, so the Act is not the only text that may apply to a deployer. Whether a particular image tool performs standard editing, whether a post concerns a matter of public interest, or whether a given watermark is enough are questions the Act answers only in general terms, and that the Commission's guidelines, the code and, in the end, authorities and courts address. Anyone who needs an answer for their own product or publication needs legal advice, and the official texts linked here are the authority, not this summary. For how we source other pieces, see <a href={`${base}blog/lab-safety-frameworks-compared/`}>our comparison of three AI lab safety frameworks</a>, which reads primary documents the same way.</p>
  <StudioNote article={article} snapshot="14 November 2026" />
</InsightArticle>
